general_comments.body:
Yes, `onError` will be called only one time, just before sending the final error to the user. It was a design decision since the beginning:

> The onError hook lives inside the standard request pipeline and is executed every time reply.send is invoked with an Error object (but only after the customErrorHandler has finished its execution, in next we always have a default);

If we call it for *every* error, we would have run it many times with the same error.

I tested this PR. Works nicely with:

```ts
import fastify, { ContextConfigDefault, FastifyRequest } from 'fastify'
import Joi from 'joi'

const app = fastify({ logger: true })

type HelloBody = { name: string }

app.route<{ Body: HelloBody }, ContextConfigDefault, Joi.Schema>({
  method: 'POST',
  url: '/hello',
  schema: {
    body: Joi.object({
      name: Joi.string().default('World')
    })
  },
  handler: (request, reply) => {
    reply.send(`Hello, ${request.body.name}`)
  },
  validatorCompiler: ({ schema }) => (data: any) => schema.validate(data || {})
})

app.listen('3000')
```

