general_comments.body:
> I know that it's is near the impossible due to the complexity, but, there is a way to define the response type after a res.code() is set? By default, the response should be the schema.response['200'], however in this case for instance: res.send(42) is matching a 400 status code that is not sent in fact.

@RafaelGSS Hi! I think there is a way to infer a default `200` type that could be remapped when the user calls either `status(...)` or `code(...)`, but this only works when sending responses via `send(...)`. I haven't provided this functionality in this PR as there is a need to support `return` type inference; and haven't found a good solution to remapping return types within the body of routes. So for example...

### Possible

```typescript
server.get('/', {
   schema: {
     response: {
       200: Type.Number(), // default is number
       500: Type.String()
     }
   }
}, (_, reply) => { 
   
   reply.code(500).send('error')
   //   ^         ^
   //   |         |
   //   |         +- ok: send(...) mapped to string
   //   |
   //   +- remapping Reply context to string here
})
```

### Not Possible

```typescript
server.get('/', {
   schema: {
     response: {
       200: Type.Number(), // default is number
       500: Type.String()
     }
   }
}, (_, reply) => { 
   
   reply.code(500)
   //   ^         
   //   |
   //   +- remapping Reply context to string here
   
   return 'error' // error: expect number
})
```
It's really the `return` type requirement that's prevented me from including narrowing in this PR. By inferring a `union` of all possible status codes, this seemed like a reasonable middle ground to get moderate type safety by allowing the user to return one of the possible status code responses only. For an example of possible future narrowing, have quick look at the demo located [here](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-UNFBUhCmM2pa0221V6t11CuNTeHwM1ikriycGjWzCAEZph0bbk8TY6FAwlIIH4YEgYFY4JJXBR+ulMO7XL7s2R3lg07X6yh1AAWaaaNNkDNZnOx4fjIuRUtQcthUeTGt1htNihV+DNjDMsid-WrHuptCbocR8fpzOEGdhRhzxgLuBLlfd9ZbfHcODASm7nGOpdimfYXoO6jXhOU73n0MZhPwcAANRwNapx+sCEAANZhAAhKIpxAA) which partially implements narrowing for `.send(...)` only.

@RafaelGSS There was actually one other thing that was holding back an implementation of type narrowing, and that was the way the `Reply` type is currently defined as a generic argument. So currently the following is possible. 

```typescript
server.get<{
   Reply: string | number
}>('/', {
   schema: {
      response: {
          200: { type: 'string' },
          500: { type: 'number' }
      }
   }
}, (_, reply) => { ... })
```
But to get the inference logic lining up, there may be a need to constrain the `Reply` specifically to type `{[statusCode: number]: unknown}`; which would change how the `Reply` generic argument is defined....
```typescript
server.get<{
   Reply: {
      200: string,
      500: number
   }
}>('/', {
   schema: {
      response: {
          200: { type: 'string' },
          500: { type: 'number' }
      }
   }
}, (_, reply) => { ... })
```
The benefit here is that type narrowing could work with and without Type Providers (which is ideal). However I think the changes required to enable this might be a bit too far reaching for the version 4 release. 

As mentioned, this PR aligns to the former (i.e. unions) rather than latter, and while there might be a potential to implement the narrowing for `send()` only, I do think it would be worth constraining the `Reply` type first as implementing narrowing on providers without this constraint would result in two distinct inference strategies. I'm getting the sense it's probably best to defer for this PR, but open to your thoughts!

